<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ronin">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rōnin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ronin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A rōnin ('drifter' or 'wandering man') was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of feudal and early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> who had no lord or master, having lost him through death or through the loss of his favour or legal privilege. The word first named serfs who had fled their master's land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> and Heian periods. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600 confiscated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> made many samurai rōnin, and as many as a hundred thousand fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka">Siege of Osaka</a>. The Edo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> forbade them new masters until the Keian Uprising of 1651, and many became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, bodyguards or criminals; the status ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a> abolished the samurai class in the 19th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 18th century rich commoners bought their way in by paying a poor retainer of the shogun to adopt a son; a samurai who lost his lord became a masterless <b>rōnin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Social Hierarchy of Tokugawa Japan</a></li><li><blockquote>A band of <b>rōnin</b> led by Ōishi Yoshio, chamberlain of the Akō Domain, killed Kira Yoshinaka, the adversary of their late master Asano Naganori.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-31">31 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rōnin</b> samurai of the Mito and Satsuma domains killed Ii Naosuke, Chief Minister of the Tokugawa shogunate, outside the Sakurada Gate of Edo Castle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-24">24 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
